export const specURL='https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/International%20GCSE/Chemistry/2017/specification-and-sample-assessments/international-gcse-chemistry-2017-specification.pdf';
// Original revision notes; a study companion, not Pearson examination material.
// title | strand | reference | explanation | retrieval question | answer | misconception
const rows=[
['Particles & changes of state','Principles','1.1–1.4','Solids have closely packed particles vibrating about fixed positions. Liquid particles are close together and move past each other. Gas particles are widely separated and move randomly. Heating increases average kinetic energy. During a change of state, energy changes attractions between particles; temperature stays constant for a pure substance at constant pressure. Diffusion is net spreading caused by random motion.','Why does a gas fill its container?','Its particles move randomly in all directions and are widely spaced.','Particles themselves do not expand when a substance is heated.'],
['Solubility','Principles','1.5C–1.7C','A saturated solution contains the maximum amount of dissolved solute at that temperature. Solubility is the mass of solute that dissolves in 100 g of solvent. To measure it, obtain a saturated sample at a controlled temperature, weigh it, evaporate the solvent and weigh the dry solute. Subtract to find solvent mass. Cooling a hot saturated solution often produces crystals.','What is solubility if 12 g dissolves in 40 g of water?','30 g per 100 g of water.','The denominator is solvent mass, not solution mass.'],
['Separation & chromatography','Principles','1.8–1.13','An element contains one type of atom; a compound contains chemically joined elements in a fixed ratio. Mixtures can be separated physically. Filter insoluble solids from liquids. Crystallise a dissolved solid. Simple distillation collects a solvent; fractional distillation separates miscible liquids with different boiling points. In chromatography, substances separate by different attractions to stationary and mobile phases. Rf = distance travelled by spot / distance travelled by solvent front.','Why draw a chromatography baseline in pencil?','Graphite does not dissolve and travel with the solvent as ink might.','Keep the baseline above the solvent and compare Rf values under the same conditions.'],
['Atoms & isotopes','Principles','1.14–1.17','Protons have relative mass 1 and charge +1; neutrons mass 1 and charge 0; electrons negligible mass and charge −1. Atomic number is the proton count. Mass number counts protons plus neutrons. Isotopes have the same proton count but different neutron counts. Relative atomic mass is a weighted mean relative to one twelfth of a carbon-12 atom.','An atom has atomic number 17 and mass number 37. How many neutrons?','20 neutrons: 37 − 17.','A relative atomic mass can be non-integer because it is an average.'],
['The Periodic Table','Principles','1.18–1.24','Elements are ordered by atomic number. A period indicates the number of occupied electron shells. For the first 20 elements use shell arrangements such as sodium 2,8,1 and calcium 2,8,8,2. Main-group elements in a group have equal outer-electron counts and similar reactions. Metals are generally on the left; non-metals on the right. Noble gases have full outer shells and are very unreactive.','What is the electron arrangement of calcium?','2,8,8,2.','Helium has a full first shell of two electrons.'],
['Formulae & equations','Principles','1.25–1.26, 1.39','Conserve each element when balancing equations: change coefficients, never formula subscripts. State symbols are (s), (l), (g) and (aq). Ionic formulae have zero overall charge: Al³⁺ and O²⁻ combine as Al₂O₃. Relative formula mass is the sum of relative atomic masses. A balanced equation gives mole ratios, not equal masses. Ionic equations omit spectator ions.','What is the formula of magnesium nitrate?','Mg(NO3)2.','Brackets are needed when there is more than one polyatomic ion.'],
['Moles & reacting quantities','Principles','1.27–1.29, 1.31–1.36','Amount n = mass / molar mass. Use coefficients to convert moles between reactants and products. The limiting reactant is consumed first; the other is in excess. Determine a metal oxide formula by comparing moles of metal with moles of oxygen gained, then finding the simplest whole-number ratio. Repeat heating and weighing to constant mass for reliable results.','How many moles are in 4.8 g of Mg (Ar = 24)?','0.20 mol.','Compare moles divided by coefficients to identify a limiting reactant.'],
['Yield, gases & concentration','Principles','1.30, 1.34C–1.35C','Percentage yield = actual yield / theoretical yield × 100. At room temperature and pressure one mole of gas occupies approximately 24 dm³ (24 000 cm³). Concentration in mol/dm³ = moles / volume in dm³. Convert cm³ to dm³ by dividing by 1000. A low yield can result from incomplete reaction, side reactions or loss during transfer and separation.','How many moles are in 25 cm³ of 0.20 mol/dm³ solution?','0.0050 mol.','24 dm³ is the school-level molar gas volume at room temperature and pressure, not at every temperature.'],
['Ionic bonding','Principles','1.37–1.43','Ionic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. Metals lose electrons to form cations; non-metals gain electrons to form anions. Ionic compounds form giant lattices with high melting points. They conduct electricity when molten or dissolved because ions can move; solid ions are fixed in place. Show transferred electrons clearly in dot-and-cross diagrams.','Why does molten sodium chloride conduct?','Its charged ions can move and carry charge.','Electrons are transferred in ionic bond formation; mobile ions carry current through the liquid.'],
['Covalent structures','Principles','1.44–1.51','A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons attracted to both nuclei. Simple molecular substances usually have low melting and boiling points because intermolecular attractions are weak. Diamond is a rigid giant covalent network. Graphite has layers, weak forces between layers and delocalised electrons that conduct electricity. C₆₀ fullerene is molecular. Silicon dioxide forms a giant covalent structure.','Why does diamond have a high melting point?','Many strong covalent bonds must be broken in its giant network.','Melting a molecular substance usually overcomes intermolecular forces, not its covalent bonds.'],
['Metallic bonding','Principles','1.52C–1.54C','Metallic bonding is electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons. Mobile electrons carry electrical charge and transfer thermal energy. Layers of metal ions can slide while attraction is maintained, making metals malleable and ductile. Different-sized atoms in an alloy disrupt regular layers and hinder sliding, so alloys are often harder.','What carries electrical charge in a metal?','Delocalised electrons.','A metal is not a collection of independent molecules.'],
['Electrolysis','Principles','1.55C–1.60C','Electrolysis uses direct current to decompose an ionic substance. Cations move to the negative cathode and gain electrons (reduction); anions move to the positive anode and lose electrons (oxidation). Molten binary compounds give the metal and non-metal. In aqueous solutions hydrogen may form instead of a reactive metal. With inert electrodes, concentrated halide solutions can give halogen at the anode; otherwise oxygen is commonly produced. Copper electrodes can dissolve and plate copper.','At which electrode does reduction occur?','The cathode.','Aqueous and molten electrolytes can give different products.'],
['Group 1','Inorganic','2.1–2.4C','Lithium, sodium and potassium react with water to form an alkaline metal hydroxide solution and hydrogen. Reactivity increases down the group. The outer electron is farther from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily despite increased nuclear charge. Predictions should use the group trend, with observations distinguished from explanations.','What gas forms when sodium reacts with water?','Hydrogen.','These reactions are represented virtually; the bench does not model their violent kinetics.'],
['Group 7','Inorganic','2.5–2.8C','At room temperature chlorine is a pale green gas, bromine a red-brown liquid and iodine a grey-black solid. Melting and boiling points increase down the group. Reactivity decreases down the group because gaining an electron becomes harder as distance and shielding increase. A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from its halide solution.','Will chlorine displace bromine from potassium bromide?','Yes: Cl2 + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br2.','A halide ion is different from the halogen molecule.'],
['Air & combustion','Inorganic','2.9–2.14','Dry air is approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.9% argon and about 0.04% carbon dioxide. React oxygen with excess material and compare gas volumes after cooling at equal pressure. Percentage oxygen = volume decrease / starting volume × 100. Combustion forms oxides. Thermal decomposition of many metal carbonates gives a metal oxide and carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide absorbs infrared radiation and contributes to the greenhouse effect.','Why cool the apparatus before reading the final gas volume?','To compare volumes at the same temperature.','Gas volume changes with temperature even without a change in amount.'],
['Reactivity & redox','Inorganic','2.15–2.21','The course reactivity order is K, Na, Li, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Cu, Ag, Au. Metals above hydrogen can release hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric or sulfuric acid. More reactive metals displace less reactive metals from suitable salt solutions. Oxidation is electron loss or oxygen gain; reduction is electron gain or oxygen loss. Oxidising agents accept electrons; reducing agents donate electrons. Rust requires both oxygen and water.','Is iron oxidised when Fe becomes Fe2+?','Yes; iron loses two electrons.','Nitric acid with metals is outside the simple hydrogen-producing acid model.'],
['Metals, extraction & corrosion','Inorganic','2.18–2.27C','Metals below carbon can often be extracted by reduction with carbon or carbon monoxide; highly reactive metals require electrolysis of molten compounds. Aluminium is light and corrosion-resistant due to its oxide layer; copper conducts well and is ductile. Steel is an iron alloy. Barriers keep out air and water. Zinc galvanising also provides sacrificial protection because zinc is more reactive than iron.','Why can zinc protect scratched galvanised iron?','Zinc oxidises preferentially and provides sacrificial protection.','Painting protects only while the barrier remains intact.'],
['Acids, bases & indicators','Inorganic','2.28–2.38','Acids donate protons and produce H⁺ ions in water. Bases accept protons; soluble bases are alkalis and provide OH⁻ ions. Neutralisation forms salt and water. Universal indicator estimates pH. Phenolphthalein is colourless in acid and pink in alkali; methyl orange is red in acid and yellow in alkali. Litmus is red in acid and blue in alkali. Concentration and acid strength describe different properties.','What is the net ionic equation for strong acid–alkali neutralisation?','H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H2O(l).','A dilute strong acid is not the same as a weak acid.'],
['Titration','Inorganic','2.33C, 2.40C','Use a pipette for a fixed volume in a conical flask and a burette for a measured variable volume. Rinse each with the solution it will hold. Swirl while adding titrant; add dropwise near the endpoint. Read the meniscus at eye level and subtract initial from final reading. Repeat for concordant titres and average the concordant results, excluding the rough trial. Use the equation mole ratio to calculate an unknown concentration.','Why use only a few drops of indicator?','To limit its effect on the reaction and see the endpoint clearly.','The endpoint is an indicator change; the equivalence point is the stoichiometric amount.'],
['Salts & solubility rules','Inorganic','2.34–2.43C','Common sodium, potassium, ammonium salts and all nitrates dissolve. Common chlorides dissolve except silver and lead(II) chlorides; common sulfates except barium, calcium and lead(II) sulfates. Common carbonates and hydroxides are mostly insoluble; sodium and potassium hydroxides dissolve and calcium hydroxide is slightly soluble. For copper sulfate crystals, react excess CuO with warm dilute sulfuric acid, filter, partly evaporate, cool, filter crystals and dry. For an insoluble salt, mix suitable solutions then filter, wash and dry.','Why add excess copper(II) oxide in salt preparation?','To use up all the acid; unreacted insoluble oxide can be filtered off.','Do not evaporate hydrated copper sulfate crystals to dryness.'],
['Chemical analysis','Inorganic','2.44–2.50','Hydrogen pops with a lighted splint; oxygen relights a glowing splint; carbon dioxide clouds limewater; ammonia turns damp red litmus blue; chlorine bleaches damp litmus. Flame colours: lithium red, sodium yellow, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, copper blue-green. NaOH gives blue Cu²⁺, green Fe²⁺ and red-brown Fe³⁺ hydroxide precipitates. Warm ammonium with NaOH for ammonia. Acidified silver nitrate tests halides: chloride white, bromide cream, iodide yellow. Acidified barium chloride tests sulfate with a white precipitate.','Why acidify a halide sample with dilute nitric acid before adding silver nitrate?','To remove interfering ions such as carbonate without adding chloride.','A white precipitate alone is insufficient: reagent and conditions matter.'],
['Water tests','Inorganic','2.49–2.50','Anhydrous copper(II) sulfate turns from white to blue in the presence of water. This shows water is present, not that a sample is pure. A pure water sample has a sharp boiling point of 100°C or melting point of 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure. Dissolved impurities typically alter melting and boiling behaviour.','Does a positive anhydrous copper sulfate test prove water is pure?','No. It proves the presence of water.','Boiling point depends on external pressure.'],
['Energetics','Physical','3.1–3.8','Exothermic reactions transfer energy to surroundings; endothermic changes take energy in. Q = mcΔT estimates energy transferred to water, using c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹. Divide by reacting moles and by 1000 for kJ/mol; an exothermic reaction has negative ΔH. Bond breaking takes energy and bond making releases energy. Estimate ΔH = total bonds broken − total bonds formed. Heat loss and incomplete combustion can reduce a measured temperature rise.','What is Q for 100 g of water warming by 5°C?','2090 J.','The reaction enthalpy sign is opposite to the heat gained by the water.'],
['Reaction rates','Physical','3.9–3.16','Rate is change in amount per unit time. A steeper product–time graph indicates a faster rate; a plateau shows no further product. Greater concentration or gas pressure increases collision frequency. Smaller solid particles expose more surface. Higher temperature increases collision frequency and the fraction of particles exceeding activation energy. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy and remains chemically unchanged. Control variables when comparing conditions.','Why does a catalyst increase rate?','It provides a pathway with a lower activation energy.','A catalyst does not increase the final yield of an irreversible reaction with fixed limiting amounts.'],
['Reversible changes & equilibrium','Physical','3.17–3.22C','A reversible reaction can proceed both ways. Hydrated copper sulfate is blue; heating removes water and gives white anhydrous salt, which turns blue again on adding water. Dynamic equilibrium requires a closed system: forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates, with constant concentrations. Increased temperature favours the endothermic direction; increased pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles. A catalyst speeds both directions without changing equilibrium composition.','Are reactant and product concentrations necessarily equal at equilibrium?','No. Their concentrations are constant; the two reaction rates are equal.','Equilibrium is dynamic, not a stopped reaction.'],
['Organic foundations','Organic','4.1–4.9','Hydrocarbons contain only carbon and hydrogen. A homologous series has a common functional group and general formula, similar reactions and gradual physical trends; successive members differ by CH₂. Structural formulae show connectivity; displayed formulae show every atom and bond. Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different structural formulae. Name simple chains using meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-.','What is a structural isomer?','A compound with the same molecular formula but a different structural formula.','Same molecular formula does not mean identical chemical structure.'],
['Crude oil & fuels','Organic','4.10–4.18','Crude oil is a mixture of hydrocarbons, separated by fractional distillation. Larger molecules generally have higher boiling points, greater viscosity and lower flammability. Complete combustion gives carbon dioxide and water. Incomplete combustion may form toxic carbon monoxide and carbon particulates. Sulfur impurities produce sulfur dioxide; high engine temperatures form nitrogen oxides. Cracking breaks long-chain alkanes into shorter alkanes and alkenes using heat and a catalyst.','Why do different fractions separate in a fractionating column?','They have different boiling ranges and condense at different heights.','Fractional distillation is a physical separation; cracking is a chemical reaction.'],
['Alkanes','Organic','4.19–4.21','Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons with only single carbon–carbon bonds and general formula CnH2n+2. They burn in oxygen. With a halogen under ultraviolet light, substitution replaces a hydrogen atom with a halogen atom. Methane plus chlorine can form chloromethane and hydrogen chloride; further substitution is possible.','What is the molecular formula of pentane?','C5H12.','Substitution needs ultraviolet light in the methane–chlorine reaction.'],
['Alkenes','Organic','4.22–4.25','Alkenes contain a carbon–carbon double bond and are unsaturated. Acyclic alkenes with one double bond have general formula CnH2n. Bromine water changes from orange to colourless in an addition reaction at the double bond. Hydrogen addition forms an alkane; steam addition can form an alcohol under suitable catalytic conditions. The double bond also allows addition polymerisation.','What observation distinguishes ethene from ethane with bromine water?','Ethene decolourises bromine water; ethane does not under normal test conditions.','Addition uses the double bond; it does not replace a hydrogen atom.'],
['Alcohols','Organic','4.26–4.32C','Alcohols contain the −OH functional group. Ethanol can be made by fermentation of glucose with yeast, without oxygen, at a warm controlled temperature. C6H12O6 → 2C2H5OH + 2CO2. Industrial hydration of ethene uses steam and a catalyst. Ethanol burns and can be oxidised to ethanoic acid. Compare renewable feedstocks, batch versus continuous processing, purity, rate and operating conditions.','Why is oxygen excluded during fermentation?','To favour anaerobic conversion of glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide.','Yeast enzymes can be denatured at excessive temperatures.'],
['Carboxylic acids','Organic','4.33C–4.37C','Carboxylic acids contain the −COOH group. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid: only some molecules ionise in water. It reacts with metals, bases and carbonates to make ethanoate salts. Oxidation of ethanol can produce ethanoic acid. Ethanoic acid plus ethanol forms an ester and water in a reversible acid-catalysed reaction.','Which gas forms when ethanoic acid reacts with sodium carbonate?','Carbon dioxide.','Weak refers to partial ionisation, not low concentration.'],
['Esters','Organic','4.38C–4.43C','Esters contain the −COO− linkage and often have fruity smells. An alcohol reacts with a carboxylic acid, with an acid catalyst and warming, to form an ester plus water. Ethanol and ethanoic acid form ethyl ethanoate. The alcohol supplies the first part of the name and the acid supplies the ending. Esterification is reversible; an equilibrium mixture contains reactants as well as products.','Name the ester formed from ethanol and ethanoic acid.','Ethyl ethanoate.','Use a warm water bath for flammable organic liquids in a supervised laboratory.'],
['Polymers','Organic','4.44–4.50C','Addition polymerisation joins alkene monomers: the double bond opens and the carbon backbone contains single bonds. Show a repeat unit with bonds extending through brackets and n outside. Condensation polymerisation joins monomers with two functional groups and eliminates small molecules. A diol and dicarboxylic acid can form a polyester and water. Many addition polymers resist biodegradation; disposal choices include recycling and combustion, each with tradeoffs.','What small molecule can be eliminated when a polyester forms from a diol and dicarboxylic acid?','Water.','Addition polymerisation does not release a small-molecule byproduct.'],
['Practical skills & uncertainty','Principles','Experimental skills','Identify the independent, dependent and control variables. Repeat measurements to assess repeatability and identify anomalies. Use apparatus with suitable resolution, read scales at eye level and state units. Accuracy is closeness to the true value; precision is small spread. Calculate means from appropriate repeat values and explain exclusions. Evaluate a method using a specific source of error, its effect and a practical improvement.','Does repeating a measurement remove a systematic error?','No. It can improve the estimate of a mean but does not remove the bias.','A conclusion should follow the evidence, not the expected answer.']
];
export const lessons=rows.map((r,i)=>({id:'lesson-'+i,title:r[0],strand:r[1],ref:r[2],text:r[3],question:r[4],answer:r[5],tip:r[6]}));
const practicalRows=[
 ['titration','A drop changes everything.','Acid–alkali titration','2.33C','Inorganic','Find the equivalence point and follow a strong acid–strong base pH curve.','Measure acid with a pipette.|Fill the burette with standard alkali and record its reading.|Swirl; approach the endpoint dropwise.|Repeat and average concordant titres.','Acid concentration and pipetted volume|Volume of alkali added|Temperature and indicator','H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)','Why use a white tile?','It makes the indicator colour change easier to see.'],
 ['rates','Small chips. Big difference.','Rates: marble & acid','3.15','Physical','Compare concentration, temperature and surface area using a gas-volume curve.','Use the same mass of calcium carbonate for comparisons.|Add measured dilute acid and start timing immediately.|Record gas volume at regular intervals.|Compare initial gradients and final gas volumes.','Surface area or concentration|CO₂ volume per second|Mass, temperature, apparatus','CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + CO₂ + H₂O','Why keep marble mass constant?','To compare one independent variable at a time.'],
 ['peroxide','A faster way forward.','Catalysts & peroxide','3.16','Physical','Explore how a catalyst changes rate while leaving the limiting yield unchanged.','Measure a fixed peroxide concentration and volume.|Add an equal mass of the chosen solid.|Collect oxygen over time.|Compare slopes and verify the solid remains unchanged.','Catalyst|O₂ volume per second|Peroxide concentration, volume, temperature','2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂','Does a catalyst increase final oxygen yield?','No; the same limiting amount gives the same final yield.'],
 ['chromatography','Separate the spectrum.','Paper chromatography','1.13','Principles','Move the solvent front and compare separated spots and Rf values.','Draw a pencil baseline above the solvent.|Place a small sample spot on the baseline.|Let solvent travel up the paper.|Mark the solvent front and measure both distances from the baseline.','Solvent or sample|Spot distance / solvent-front distance|Paper, temperature, solvent','Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance','Why keep the baseline above the solvent?','To prevent the sample dissolving into the solvent reservoir.'],
 ['electrolysis','Let the ions lead.','Electrolysis','1.60C','Principles','Choose an electrolyte and electrode type, then inspect products and half-equations.','Use a suitable ionic electrolyte.|Connect electrodes to a direct-current supply.|Collect products separately at each electrode.|Use appropriate gas tests and compare electrode changes.','Electrolyte and electrodes|Electrode products|Current, time, temperature','Reduction at cathode · oxidation at anode','Which electrode attracts cations?','The negative cathode.'],
 ['energetics','Feel the energy shift.','Temperature changes','3.8','Physical','Model heat transfer for neutralisation, displacement, dissolving and combustion.','Measure initial temperature and quantities.|Mix or heat the measured water sample.|Record the maximum or minimum temperature.|Calculate Q = mcΔT and discuss heat loss.','Type and amount of reaction|Temperature change|Water mass, insulation, apparatus','Q = mcΔT · ΔH = −Q/n','Why use an insulated cup?','To reduce heat exchange with the surroundings.'],
 ['solubility','Find the saturation point.','Solubility investigation','1.7C','Principles','Use an explicitly illustrative solute to compare saturation and crystallisation.','Prepare a saturated solution at a measured temperature.|Weigh a sample and evaporate its solvent.|Weigh dry solute to constant mass.|Calculate solubility per 100 g of solvent.','Temperature|Mass dissolved per 100 g water|Solute and solvent','solubility = mass solute / mass solvent × 100','Which mass is in the denominator?','The solvent mass.'],
 ['oxide','Weigh the invisible.','Formula of a metal oxide','1.36','Principles','Use magnesium and oxygen masses to infer an empirical formula.','Weigh a clean, dry crucible with its lid.|Add magnesium and reweigh.|Heat with the lid lifted briefly to admit oxygen.|Cool, weigh and repeat heating to constant mass.','Mass of metal|Mass increase|Crucible, complete reaction, cooling','2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO','Why keep a lid on the crucible?','To reduce loss of magnesium oxide while still admitting oxygen periodically.'],
 ['oxygen','Discover the fifth of air.','Oxygen in air','2.14','Inorganic','Compare starting and final air volumes after oxygen is removed by excess metal.','Measure an initial volume of air.|Pass the air over excess heated copper or use damp iron wool.|Continue until volume stops changing.|Cool to the initial temperature before reading final volume.','Oxygen removal|Volume decrease|Temperature, pressure, initial air volume','2Cu + O₂ → 2CuO','Why use excess metal?','To ensure all available oxygen can react.'],
 ['metals','Put reactivity to the test.','Metals & dilute acids','2.21','Inorganic','Compare magnesium, zinc, iron and copper in dilute hydrochloric acid.','Use equal exposed surface areas of metals.|Add equal measured acid concentrations and volumes.|Collect hydrogen over time.|Compare observations and infer a reactivity order.','Metal|Hydrogen production rate|Acid concentration, volume, temperature','Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂','Why does copper give no hydrogen with dilute HCl?','It is below hydrogen in the reactivity series.'],
 ['crystals','From black powder to blue.','Copper sulfate crystals','2.42','Inorganic','Calculate the limiting reagent, filter excess oxide and grow hydrated crystals.','Warm dilute sulfuric acid; add CuO in excess.|Filter off unreacted black copper(II) oxide.|Gently evaporate some water, then cool.|Filter crystals and dry between filter papers.','Reactant amounts|Potential crystal yield|Acid concentration, temperature, isolation','CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O','Why avoid heating to dryness?','Hydrated crystals can lose water of crystallisation.'],
 ['precipitation','Make the insoluble visible.','Lead(II) sulfate','2.43C','Inorganic','Predict a white precipitate and calculate its theoretical yield.','Mix measured soluble lead(II) nitrate and sodium sulfate solutions.|Filter the white lead(II) sulfate.|Wash residue with distilled water.|Dry the solid; collect lead-containing waste appropriately.','Reactant amounts|Mass of precipitate|Concentration, washing, drying','Pb(NO₃)₂ + Na₂SO₄ → PbSO₄ + 2NaNO₃','Why wash the precipitate?','To remove soluble salts adhering to it.'],
 ['ester','Meet the fruity side of chemistry.','Making an ester','4.43C','Organic','Explore reactant ratios in a qualitative esterification model.','Combine a carboxylic acid and alcohol.|Use an acid catalyst and controlled warming in a water bath.|Allow esterification to proceed.|Identify the ester from its reactants and discuss reversibility.','Alcohol and acid amounts|Equilibrium ester amount|Catalyst, temperature, time','CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O','What does an acid catalyst change?','The rate of reaching equilibrium, not the equilibrium position.'],
 ['analysis','Every ion leaves a clue.','Chemical tests','2.44–2.50','Inorganic','Choose an unknown ion and its reagent to practise observations and identification.','Use a fresh sample for each test.|Add the correct acid before testing anions.|Observe colour and precipitate formation.|Use an appropriate confirmatory test.','Unknown ion and reagent|Observed colour or gas|Sample volume, contamination','Identify ions from characteristic reactions','Why use fresh portions?','To prevent previous reagents interfering with later tests.'],
 ['flames','Read the colour of an element.','Flame tests','2.45–2.46','Inorganic','Inspect the five required flame-test colours.','Clean a wire loop using hydrochloric acid.|Check that it gives no colour in a non-luminous flame.|Dip into the sample and return to the flame.|Compare the observed colour with known references.','Cation|Flame colour|Clean loop, flame conditions','Li⁺ · Na⁺ · K⁺ · Ca²⁺ · Cu²⁺','Why clean the loop first?','To prevent contamination, especially from sodium.'],
 ['equilibrium','A balance that keeps moving.','Dynamic equilibrium','3.17–3.22C','Physical','Predict changes in an exothermic gas equilibrium using temperature and pressure.','Consider a sealed N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ system.|Change one condition at a time.|Predict the favoured direction.|Distinguish rate from equilibrium composition.','Temperature or pressure|Qualitative equilibrium shift|Closed system, other conditions','N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ · forward exothermic','Does higher pressure favour ammonia?','Yes; the product side has fewer moles of gas.'],
 ['particles','See chemistry in motion.','Particle explorer','1.1–1.3','Principles','Compare particle arrangement and movement in solids, liquids and gases.','Choose a state of matter.|Compare spacing and freedom of movement.|Change temperature within a state.|Explain diffusion with random motion.','State or temperature|Particle movement|Identity and number of particles','solid ⇌ liquid ⇌ gas','Do gas particles become larger when heated?','No. Their average kinetic energy increases.'],
 ['organic','Follow the functional group.','Organic reaction explorer','4.1–4.50C','Organic','Trace combustion, cracking, addition, oxidation and polymerisation.','Select a reaction family.|Inspect reactants, conditions and products.|Identify the functional group change.|Write a balanced equation where appropriate.','Reaction family|Products|Stated reaction conditions','Carbon chemistry · structure to reaction','What makes an alkene unsaturated?','Its carbon–carbon double bond.']
];
